1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,800
Mitochondria are the tiny power plants inside our cells.

2
00:00:02,800 --> 00:00:05,400
Some scientists think they run at about 50 °C,

3
00:00:05,400 --> 00:00:08,000
more than 10 degrees hotter than the rest of the cell.

4
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:12,500
Living things from hotter places build proteins that hold up to more heat.

5
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:17,000
Across 13 species, from icy seas to hot springs, the pattern holds.

6
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:21,500
So proteins working inside hot power plants should be built much tougher.

7
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:25,500
We checked public data: over 100,000 measurements, 6 species, 4 labs.

8
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:30,000
They're not tougher. They hold up about the same as the rest of the cell.

9
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,600
Two more checks agreed:

10
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:37,000
matched “sister” proteins, and an AI that reads heat-toughness from a protein's recipe.

11
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:42,000
Another possibility: maybe the whole cell gets just as hot, so all its proteins had to toughen up.

12
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:48,000
That seems unlikely. The few measurements so far show the rest of the cell warming only 1–2 degrees.

13
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:53,500
One simple lab test could settle it: switch the power plants off and see if their proteins suddenly look tougher.

14
00:00:53,500 --> 00:00:57,600
Summary and code: link in the post.

